which allows us to evaluate the uncertainty in w after we have observed D in the form of the posterior probability p(w|D). It is important to emphasize that the quantity p(D|w) is called the likelihood function when it is viewed as a function of the parameter vector w, and it expresses how probable the observed data set is for different values of w. Note that the likelihood p(D|w) is not a probability distribution over w, and its integral with respect to w does not (necessarily) equal one. Given this definition of likelihood, we can state Bayes’ theorem in words:
